# {{Name}}

A .NET 10 game generated by Obelisk CLI, using Obelisk.Vanilla {{PackageVersion}}.

## Prerequisites

.NET 10 SDK and Windows x64, or experimental macOS with a Metal-capable GPU.
On Windows, install the [Microsoft Visual C++ v14 Redistributable (x64)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/windows/latest-supported-vc-redist)
for the native SDL/Shadercross dependencies; recipients of self-contained builds
need it too.
Package restore requires a NuGet source containing Obelisk.Vanilla {{PackageVersion}}
and its dependencies. Released versions can restore from nuget.org. For an
unpublished development build, use the local feed described in the engine README.

## Build and run

```powershell
dotnet restore
dotnet build
dotnet run
# With an installed Obelisk CLI and a configured NuGet source:
obelisk run
obelisk dev
```

Move the rectangle with arrow keys. Close the window to exit. Edit Game.cs to
develop your game. No engine source checkout or ProjectReference is needed.

## Open in an editor

- VS Code: install the [C# Dev Kit](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/csharp/get-started),
  open this folder with `code .`, and run the commands above in its terminal.
- Visual Studio 2026 (18.0+) supports targeting `net10.0`: choose
  **File > Open > Project/Solution**, then select `{{Name}}.csproj`.
- Rider: choose **File > Open**, then select `{{Name}}.csproj` so Rider loads it
  as a .NET project rather than just opening the folder.

Configure the same NuGet source in the editor as for command-line restore.
When using unpublished development packages, keep the local feed available.

## Project layout

- Program.cs: Vanilla startup
- Game.cs: public game lifecycle and Canvas drawing
- {{Name}}.csproj: exact Obelisk.Vanilla package version
- obelisk.json: schema 1 project metadata for CLI development commands
- assets/: copied to build/publish output; project:/// asset root
- plugins/: copied local packages; install/remove while the process is stopped
- artifacts/: CLI locks, temporary files and generated distribution (ignored)

Plugin rendering uses public Canvas commands. See the engine README for the local
Plugin manifest and lifecycle. `obelisk run -- <arguments>` restores, builds and
executes once. `obelisk dev -- <arguments>` combines saves over a 300 ms quiet period,
stops the previous game and restarts after rebuilding. Source/configuration and
assets/plugins changes are watched; bin/obj are ignored. Fix build errors and save
again to retry. Close the game to wait for another change, or press Ctrl+C in the
CLI to stop development and release its child processes. On Windows, assigned
descendants are also stopped if the CLI is forcibly terminated.

With the game stopped, install a complete local Plugin package using
`obelisk add "C:\packages\sample"`, inspect versions with `obelisk list`, and remove
by manifest name using `obelisk remove sample`. Install/remove/build cannot overlap
another CLI run/dev or mutation; stop the other command first. Direct dotnet/.exe
processes must also be stopped before changing Plugins. Packages are trusted code;
the installer checks metadata and paths without executing them.

`obelisk build --runtime osx-arm64` publishes an experimental Apple Silicon distribution
to artifacts/build/osx-arm64. Use osx-x64 for Intel Macs (not yet certified).
Run the extensionless {{Name}} apphost on the matching Mac. Signing, notarization
and .app bundling are not included in this alpha.

`obelisk build` retains the Windows default and publishes Release self-contained Windows x64 output to
artifacts/build/win-x64. Copy that whole folder and run {{Name}}.exe on a compatible
Windows x64 machine; the recipient needs no .NET SDK or installed shared runtime.
The directory includes native dependencies, assets and Plugin packages, as well as
the engine dependency notices under `licenses/`; preserve that directory
when distributing the game. The VC++ runtime remains a target-machine prerequisite.
Rebuild replaces only output marked as owned by this CLI/project and removes stale files.
Failures preserve the previous build; save custom files outside generated output.

## Terminal debug control (alpha)

Run `obelisk run --debug-control --session mygame`, then use another terminal:

```sh
obelisk debug --session mygame pause
obelisk debug --session mygame click 320 180
obelisk debug --session mygame key press Enter
obelisk debug --session mygame step 1
obelisk debug --session mygame screenshot ./frame.png
obelisk debug --session mygame resume
obelisk debug --session mygame quit
```

`status` reports output pixel dimensions, scale, completed frames and input state.
Coordinates are output pixels from the top-left. Click/press advances two frames,
even while paused. `step` requires pause. PNG captures the last engine frame and
refuses to overwrite a file. The local endpoint is restricted to the same OS user;
normal execution does not enable it. Use matching alpha CLI and engine packages.
